Functions mlibc left unfinished, as glibc has them, each checked against
glibc 2.39 on Linux 6.8. These ended the program with "Not implemented"
or crashed when memory ran out.

Out of memory: frg::construct and construct_n give a null pointer when
the allocation fails, and fopen, fdopen, tmpfile, fmemopen,
open_memstream, fopencookie and pthread_rwlockattr_init fail with ENOMEM
then (fopen and tmpfile close what they opened), where they wrote
through the null pointer.

Streams: tmpnam takes a name only when nothing has it (it took a file it
could not open for a free name); tempnam is glibc's (TMPDIR unless the
program is set-ID, then the directory asked for, P_tmpdir and /tmp,
whichever is a directory, a prefix of up to five bytes, "file" without
one); fgetln is the BSDs'; <stdio_ext.h>'s __freadable, __fwritable,
__freadptr, __freadptrinc and _flushlbf work.

Clocks: adjtimex, ntp_adjtime and clock_adjtime tell what Linux tells
of a clock no time server synchronized (TIME_ERROR, STA_UNSYNC), setting
anything is EPERM, and clocks other than CLOCK_REALTIME are EOPNOTSUPP
or EINVAL, as there.

Names: dn_expand, dn_comp, ns_initparse, ns_parserr and
ns_name_uncompress read and write the names of DNS messages as glibc
does, its escapes included; res_query fails with NO_RECOVERY, as there
is no name server to ask yet. ether_ntoa writes lower-case hex without
leading zeros and ether_aton reads one or two digits a byte, with the
_r forms; inet_makeaddr, inet_netof and inet_lnaof are the classful
ones; getdomainname gives uname's domain ("(none)") and setdomainname is
EPERM.

The rest: parse_printf_format gives glibc's argument types, positional
arguments included; backtrace_symbols gives glibc's lines in one block
the caller frees; recvmmsg and sendmmsg send and receive one message at
a time, with MSG_WAITFORONE and the timeout; prlimit works on the calling
process; pthread_rwlockattr_setkind_np and getkind_np keep the kind;
semop is ENOSYS as System V semaphores are not there; remap_file_pages
is ENOSYS; getsgnam_r finds no group (ENOENT). Meant for upstream.

